## TRAP for the next lane (cost an afternoon)

F5 compares the two scenes' embedded `graph_hash` BYTE-EXACTLY. Rule-0
admissions change the store hash, so `.tmp/coupled-native/scene.json` COPIED
from an older clone (old hash `5cb2a185...`) fails
`f5_initial_status_byte_identical` against a scene compiled from the admitted
store (new hash `3463194a...`) - and the only symptom is the refusal message
landing on stdout while the runner tails stderr. RECOMPILE BOTH SCENES from the
current store after any admission: `python -m
tools.science_funnel.coupled_scene` then `python -m
tools.science_funnel.coupled_free_scene`. Graph-hash equality across the two
scenes is a feature, not a bug - it pins the whole graph generation.

## FINAL RECEIPTS (reference box i9-13900K, 3 consecutive qualification runs on
the final binary, F1-F8 passing in the same runs, frozen qualified anchors
reproduced bit-exactly: gap 4.14747585802955e-07, heat 0.06656390658451124;
oracle worst 5.47e-12 abs / 3.64e-11 rel over 868 comparisons)

```
run  mounted ms  free ms   ratio
 1     0.8208    2.2354    2.72
 2     0.8164    2.2269    2.73
 3     0.8332    2.2943    2.75
median 0.8208    2.2269    2.73
```

F1: 2.75 <= 5x AND 2.2943 <= 3.33. F2: median 2.73 <= 3x. F4 envelope: seated
mu=0.6 profile 2.40 ms = 72% of the 300 Hz tick. The 7-run development table
(median 2.03x) and these 3 receipts (median 2.73x, heavier background load)
bracket the ratio between two load regimes with no solver change - itself
evidence for the 5x single-window headroom.

